WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Google just made it possible to create and edit real documents inside an ai chat. School reports can now be finished in the same window used to plan them. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 212. Claude checking its own replies, a free text to video experiment, and quick bits come after this. Gemini, Google's Chatbot can now build a Google doc, a slide deck, a PDF, or a spreadsheet, then keep changing that file when you send another message. A PDF is that familiar file you open when

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:31.780
and someone sends a flyer or a form.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.280
Google Docs is the online writing app

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.060
many people already use for homework.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:39.760
The Verge reports the new options also covered Google Sheets,

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.960
Slide Decks, Markdown Files, TXT Files, and Code.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:46.400
Markdown is a simple writing format

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.980
that uses plain type symbols for headings and lists.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.720
A TXT file is just basic, unformatted text,

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:54.900
and Code means computer instructions.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.920
PDF preview and editing work on the web

00:00:57.920 --> 00:00:59.120
and on the mobile apps.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.200
The other file types currently work only on the web.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.980
Picture a group chat where one person can actually produce the poster while everyone else

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:07.740
is still talking.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:13.040
You use to copy words out of a chatbot and paste them into another tab, like carrying

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.220
water in a cup instead of turning on a tap.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:20.360
Now the file lives on the same bench as the conversation, so you never lose your place.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:24.980
Talk to Gemini the way you would talk to a classmate who both writes the first draft

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:26.980
and then fixes the heading when you ask.

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:32.480
People already use Google tools for homework, group projects, and creative work, so this

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:34.420
sits inside a workspace they already know.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:39.920
A student can brainstorm an essay, generate the outline, and turn it into a formatted document

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:40.940
in one conversation.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:46.260
Anyone making posters, resumes, or presentations gets a faster back and forth.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:51.320
The feature rolled out starting today and is available to all Gemini users at no extra

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:51.780
cost.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:56.520
To try it, open Google's Gemini website, start a new chat, and type this.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:01.440
Create a one-page Google Doc about why Bs are important with a simple title and three

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:02.000
bullet points.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:07.400
Then send a follow-up like, add a picture description at the bottom, and watch the file change

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:08.580
instead of starting over.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:12.400
You can also ask for a short slide deck for a class presentation.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:16.600
Then say, change the second slide to have two columns instead of one.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:21.331
Checking your own work after a test is a habit most people already have.

00:02:21.811 --> 00:02:26.911
Claude, another chatbot, now does a version of that glance after every single reply it gives.

00:02:27.511 --> 00:02:31.751
A post on X described the system as self-assessment added to Claude in June.

00:02:32.371 --> 00:02:38.531
Right after it answers, the model looks at the whole conversation and asks whether the reply could be harmful or against the rules.

00:02:39.131 --> 00:02:41.511
It then writes a short note about what it just said.

00:02:41.511 --> 00:02:46.331
Because Claude has already read every message in the chat, it can spot patterns that simpler

00:02:46.331 --> 00:02:50.371
filters might miss, such as someone trying to get advice on something unsafe.

00:02:51.011 --> 00:02:54.871
Treat those older filters as a locked door with a fixed list of forbidden rooms.

00:02:55.451 --> 00:03:00.651
Self-assessment is closer to a student rereading a text before hitting send, using judgment

00:03:00.651 --> 00:03:03.811
about the whole thread rather than a static list of bad topics.

00:03:04.491 --> 00:03:08.131
Claude now gives this preliminary safety assessment right after it responds.

00:03:08.131 --> 00:03:12.751
bonds. The technique has already surfaced misuse that earlier safeguards were not designed

00:03:12.751 --> 00:03:17.971
to catch. The check happens inside the same model that just answered, so it stays efficient

00:03:17.971 --> 00:03:23.111
even as conversations grow longer. As the underlying model improves, these self-checks

00:03:23.111 --> 00:03:27.811
are expected to get better too, since they rely on the model's own growing judgment rather

00:03:27.811 --> 00:03:32.671
than outside rules written by humans. The extra safety layer runs automatically in

00:03:32.671 --> 00:03:37.131
the background without slowing down normal conversations. You already know that feeling

00:03:37.131 --> 00:03:40.771
of sending a message and then noticing a tone problem two seconds later.

00:03:41.231 --> 00:03:44.791
Claude is being taught to take that second look on purpose every time

00:03:44.791 --> 00:03:47.951
and to leave a brief note on the fridge about what it found.

00:03:48.511 --> 00:03:51.931
Once you see it that way, a safety pass stops sounding like magic

00:03:51.931 --> 00:03:55.351
and starts sounding like a careful habit built into the reply itself.

00:03:56.241 --> 00:04:00.241
A free artificial intelligence video tool can turn a short-type description

00:04:00.241 --> 00:04:03.981
into a polished clip that looks like a tiny production team made it.

00:04:03.981 --> 00:04:09.301
You describe what you want, a quick explainer for a school project, a fun birthday message

00:04:09.301 --> 00:04:13.941
or a short story scene, and the tool handles the visuals, the voice, and the timing.

00:04:14.621 --> 00:04:18.521
Calling that description a prompt just means the sentence you type to tell the system

00:04:18.521 --> 00:04:19.201
what to make.

00:04:19.801 --> 00:04:24.281
Teens who want content for YouTube, TikTok, or class presentations can skip learning

00:04:24.281 --> 00:04:25.921
complicated editing software.

00:04:26.661 --> 00:04:30.221
Ordering a dish out loud and letting the kitchen plate it is the same idea.

00:04:30.221 --> 00:04:33.381
You name the scene and the generator cooks the shots.

00:04:33.981 --> 00:04:38.781
Analytics insight has a free step-by-step guide for choosing a free generator, writing

00:04:38.781 --> 00:04:40.821
a clear prompt and reviewing the output.

00:04:41.441 --> 00:04:46.261
Follow that guide in your browser, then try typing a 30-second animated video explaining

00:04:46.261 --> 00:04:49.401
how photosynthesis works with friendly cartoon characters.

00:04:50.241 --> 00:04:54.541
Photosynthesis is how plants turn light into food, and you should notice whether the

00:04:54.541 --> 00:04:57.041
clip keeps a simple beginning, middle, and end.

00:04:57.041 --> 00:05:02.421
You can also try a 15-second TikTok-style clip about your favorite game, or a simple

00:05:02.421 --> 00:05:07.441
how-to video for a hobby you enjoy, and test several ideas in one sitting because it is

00:05:07.441 --> 00:05:08.121
free to start.

00:05:09.083 --> 00:05:13.163
Christian today found that roughly half of all questions from Christian users on chat

00:05:13.163 --> 00:05:16.903
GPT are about understanding Bible passages or stories.

00:05:17.523 --> 00:05:22.863
Chat GPT is a chatbot people type questions into, and many use it to get a simple explanation

00:05:22.863 --> 00:05:26.523
of a verse or to compare translations without opening multiple books.

00:05:26.523 --> 00:05:30.663
That is personal curiosity at any hour, not only schoolwork.

00:05:31.685 --> 00:05:37.105
BLS International, which handles visa services for governments in over 100 countries, is

00:05:37.105 --> 00:05:42.505
working with signsy on tools that flag possible forged papers or deep-fake photos during visa

00:05:42.505 --> 00:05:42.885
checks.

00:05:43.425 --> 00:05:47.525
A deep-fake photo looks real but was generated or heavily altered by software.

00:05:48.145 --> 00:05:53.005
A Reddit post said the system scans submitted documents for anomalies that could point to manipulation.

00:05:53.005 --> 00:05:57.125
Then people verify those flags before any government makes a decision.

00:05:57.645 --> 00:06:01.725
Honest applicants are meant to wait less, while humans stay in charge of the important

00:06:01.725 --> 00:06:06.305
choice, the way a metal detector pings and a person still decides what happens next.

00:06:06.905 --> 00:06:07.565
And that's a wrap.

00:06:08.185 --> 00:06:10.785
